Z||000010||Toddster||06-17-2001||10:20 PM||||Seems a little pricy to me. especially since you will need braided brake lines to really help with the braking (an extra $100 bucks easily).<P>I paid $700 for an '88 Fiero last year. Gotta think that option is a wee bit more attractive.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroJoe||06-17-2001||10:26 PM||||YES!  Way out of line!!!<P>First of all, the Fiero store's kit is a much better deal.<P>Second, it doesnt look to me like you can still have your ebrake.<P>And third, if you are going to be using 4 piston calipers all around, your master cylender is probably going to do some funky stuff.  Think about it, the master cylender was designed and made to run 4 pistons total, and now your going to try and run 16 without any modifcations or adjustments??  I dont think it would work as well.<P>I really don't have much of a problem stopping all together, but brake fade is a factor that I run into once and a while.  If I were to do a brake upgrade, I'd probably buy the vented, cross drilled rotors from the Fiero store.  I'm sure I'd see a big improvement over my soild ones.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000012||skitime||06-17-2001||10:38 PM||||Wow, I have been enlighted.  I hope someone doesn't pay that.  You are right it is out of line.  Thanks for the fun we had with the thread too.||||reg||1
